%\VignetteIndexEntry{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\documentclass[nojss]{jss}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{Sweave}

\title{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\author{Vegan development team}
\Plainauthor{Vegan development team}
\Plaintitle{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\Shorttitle{Comparisons in constrained ordination}
\Keywords{constrained ordination, contrasts, permutation tests,
  pseudo-$F$, vegan}
\Plainkeywords{constrained ordination, contrasts, permutation tests,
  pseudo-F, vegan}
\Address{Vegan development team\\
  URL: \url{https://vegandevs.github.io/vegan/}}

\Abstract{
  Function \code{ordicomparisons()} specifies and tests focused comparisons
  after fitting a constrained ordination model. Function
  \code{ordicontrasts()} optionally displays the adjusted factor-level
  profiles used to define custom contrasts.
  The interface provides common sets of comparisons, user-written
  comparisons, comparisons within interactions, joint tests,
  adjustments for multiple testing, and control over the population to which
  a comparison applies. Each question is tested using additional
  constrained inertia and vegan's permutation pseudo-$F$ statistic.
  This document introduces the interface without assuming knowledge of
  contrast algebra. A technical description of the implementation is
  given in the appendix.
}

\begin{document}

\SweaveOpts{strip.white=true}
<<echo=false>>=
options(width = 72, digits = 4)
options("prompt" = "> ", "continue" = "  ")
@

\begin{center}
\footnotesize processed with vegan
\Sexpr{packageDescription("vegan", field="Version")}
in \Sexpr{R.version.string} on \today
\end{center}

\section{Comparing factor levels after fitting an ordination model}

Consider the following RDA model fitted to the Dutch dune meadow data:

<<zero-model>>=
library("vegan")
data(dune)
data(dune.env)

mod <- rda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env)
@

A permutation test of the \code{Management} term can tell us whether
the factor is associated with variation in multivariate composition.
It does not answer more focused questions such as ``does management
type HF differ from BF?''. More generally, the marginal or term-wise
tests previously available in vegan could not directly answer questions
like ``does each treatment differ from the control?'' or ``does the
treatment difference change between habitats?''

This vignette describes the new function \code{ordicomparisons()}, which
asks these questions after a model has been fitted with \code{rda()},
\code{cca()}, or \code{dbrda()}.

The function changes the focal factor to the levels named in a
comparison while retaining the rest of the fitted model. This matters
when a model contains covariates, interactions, or transformed terms.
The comparison is then tested with the same kind of permutation
pseudo-$F$ statistic used elsewhere in vegan. It is not a test of an
individual model \emph{coefficient}, and its result does not depend on the
contrast coding used to fit the factor.

The returned inertia, $R^2$, and pseudo-$F$ measure the magnitude of a
difference, but not its direction. Consequently, reversing a
comparison gives the same test. Inspect the community data, fitted
values, or ordination scores when the ecological direction of a
difference is also needed.

All the examples contained here use just 99 permutations to keep the vignette quick to
build. More permutations should normally be used in an analysis. With
99 permutations, the smallest attainable permutation $p$-value is
0.01.

\section{Pairwise comparisons of management types}

We begin by returning to the model above: a redundancy analysis in which
plant community composition is related to management type and the
numeric variable \code{A1}.

<<first-model>>=
mod <- rda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env)

# save a set of permutation indices for use throughout
set.seed(2718)
perm <- shuffleSet(nrow(dune), nset = 99)
@

The main interface is \code{ordicomparisons()}. The \code{variable}
argument names the factor whose levels we want to compare. By default,
the function tests every pair of levels, accounting for the other model
terms.

<<first-pairwise>>=
pw <- ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", permutations = perm)
pw
@

For custom questions, we can optionally inspect the adjusted profiles
underlying the contrasts with \code{ordicontrasts()}. There is one row for
each level of \code{Management}. These rows identify quantities that can
be combined in a contrast; an individual row is not itself a zero-sum
contrast.

<<first-profiles>>=
sc <- ordicontrasts(mod, "Management")
sc
@

The labels \code{b1}, \code{b2}, and so on give stable, short names to
these rows. \code{Averaged over: A1} says that each management profile
is averaged over the observed values of \code{A1}. Each row represents
a factor level with the other variables averaged or held fixed. Since
the response contains several species, there is no single response
estimate to display for each row. \code{N.reference} is the number of reference
rows used in the average, not a new sample size, and
\code{Extrapolated} warns if supplied numeric values fall outside the
fitted data range.

In the \code{pw} results, each row is a separate test. The columns have
the following meanings:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{Comparison} identifies the factor levels being compared.
  \item \code{Df} is the number of independent dimensions in the
    comparison. An ordinary pairwise comparison has one degree of
    freedom.
  \item \code{Inertia} is the variation attributed to this comparison,
    after accounting for the other differences allowed under the null
    hypothesis.
  \item \code{R2} is that inertia as a proportion of the total inertia
    in the response.
  \item \code{F} is the observed pseudo-$F$ statistic.
  \item \code{N.Perm} and \code{Pr(>F)} give the number of permutations
    and the permutation $p$-value.
\end{itemize}

The tests share the supplied permutation matrix, which makes an analysis
reproducible. Each has its own null model, which allows the other fitted
effects while assuming the requested difference is zero. They should
therefore be regarded as separate tests.

\section{Pairwise, reference, and other built-in comparisons}

The \code{comparison} argument to \code{ordicomparisons()} selects a
ready-made set of comparisons. Table \ref{tab:recipes} summarises the
available choices. The order of factor
levels is important for consecutive, reference, Helmert, and polynomial
comparisons; inspect it with \code{levels()} before using those choices.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Value & Question represented \\
\midrule
\code{"pairwise"} & Every pair of levels \\
\code{"sequential"}, \code{"consecutive"} & Each level versus the preceding level \\
\code{"reference"} & Every other level versus a reference \\
\code{"trt.vs.ctrl"} & Every treatment versus the control level \\
\code{"deviation"}, \code{"effect"} & Each level versus the equal-weight level mean \\
\code{"helmert"} & Successive Helmert comparisons \\
\code{"poly"} & Linear and higher polynomial patterns across levels \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Built-in comparisons. Names on the same row are equivalent.}
\label{tab:recipes}
\end{table}

There are no reverse variants of these built-in comparisons. Reversing a comparison
does not change its inertia, pseudo-$F$, or permutation $p$-value. When
the displayed direction is important, write the desired character
comparison explicitly.

For example, all management types can be compared with BF:

<<reference>>=
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm)
@

If the wording of the study identifies a control, \code{"trt.vs.ctrl"}
expresses the same family of questions. By default, the first fitted
factor level is the control. Use \code{relevel()} before fitting to change
the factor's reference level, or supply \code{reference} to override it
for this call. BF is already the first level of \code{Management}, so no
override is needed here.

<<control>>=
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            permutations = perm)
@

Consecutive comparisons are useful when levels have a meaningful
ordering.

<<consecutive>>=
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "sequential",
            permutations = perm)
@

Helmert and polynomial comparisons use the definitions in base R. Polynomial
comparisons are usually meaningful only when the factor levels really
describe an ordered progression. They operate on equally spaced level
positions; they do not infer numerical spacing from level labels.

\section{Specifying comparisons with equations or numeric weights}

Specific scientific questions can be written directly as character
strings. Factor-level names stand for their adjusted profiles. The
right-hand side of each equation must currently be zero.

<<specific>>=
specific <- ordicomparisons(
    mod, "Management",
    comparison = c(
        "HF - BF = 0",
        "(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"
    ),
    permutations = perm)
specific
@

The second row compares BF with the average of NM and SF. Parentheses,
addition, subtraction, unary signs, and multiplication or division by
numeric constants are supported. A level name containing spaces or
operator characters must be enclosed in backticks. Expressions are
checked to allow only these linear combinations; they cannot call R
functions.

Names can make the output easier to read.

<<named-specific>>=
ordicomparisons(
    mod, "Management",
    comparison = c(
        HF_vs_BF = "HF - BF = 0",
        managed_mean_vs_BF = "(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"
    ),
    permutations = perm)
@

You can instead supply numeric weights. A named numeric vector
defines one comparison and a matrix defines one comparison per row.
Supply one finite weight for every fitted factor level. The weights
in each row must sum to zero. Multiplying all weights in a row by the
same nonzero constant leaves the test unchanged.

<<numeric-comparisons>>=
cmat <- rbind(
    HF_vs_BF = c(BF = -1, HF = 1, NM = 0, SF = 0),
    managed_mean_vs_BF = c(BF = -1, HF = 0, NM = 0.5, SF = 0.5)
)
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = cmat,
            permutations = perm)
@

A named list may mix individual character and numeric specifications.
This can be convenient when a program constructs the comparisons one at
a time.

<<list-comparisons>>=
clist <- list(
    HF_vs_BF = "HF - BF = 0",
    SF_vs_NM = c(BF = 0, HF = 0, NM = -1, SF = 1)
)
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = clist,
            permutations = perm)
@

\section{Comparing rows of the contrast table}

The \code{Profile} column gives each row a short name: \code{b1},
\code{b2}, and so on always refer to rows of that table. In \code{sc},
the first two rows are BF and HF, so the following is the same question
as \code{"HF - BF = 0"}:

<<profile-pair>>=
ordicomparisons(sc, comparison = "b2 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = perm)
@

The notation is especially helpful for more involved questions. This
compares BF with the average of the third and fourth displayed
profiles:

<<profile-average>>=
ordicomparisons(sc, comparison = "(b3 + b4) / 2 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = perm)
@

These identifiers do not refer to model coefficients, previously
calculated comparisons, inertia values, or pseudo-$F$ statistics. The
expression defines a difference between the profiles, and the function
then tests that difference with a pseudo-$F$ statistic.

\subsection{Testing comparisons together and adjusting p-values}

Several comparison rows can be tested together with \code{joint = TRUE}.
The result is one test of the whole set, with degrees of freedom
determined by the number of independent comparisons.

<<joint>>=
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "BF", joint = TRUE,
            permutations = perm)
@

A joint test asks whether any of the specified differences is present.
It does not identify which comparison is responsible. Comparisons that
can be obtained by combining the others do not add degrees of freedom.
For example, C--B can be obtained by subtracting B--A from C--A, so
the three pairwise comparisons among A, B, and C give a joint test with
two degrees of freedom. The function determines this number automatically.

When separate tests are retained, their $p$-values can be adjusted with
any method accepted by \code{p.adjust()}. For example:

<<adjust>>=
ordicomparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "pairwise",
            adjust = "holm", permutations = perm)
@

The adjustment accounts for testing several questions together. It does
not change the individual pseudo-$F$ statistics or the unadjusted
$p$-values. Adjustments based on the largest statistic across tests are
not provided because the separate comparisons generally have different
null models.

\section{Comparisons in models with interactions}

When the model contains an interaction, a difference between treatment
levels may depend on the interacting variable. We use a small unbalanced
example with three treatments in two habitats. The response has six
species, and blocks will later be used to restrict permutations.

<<interaction-data>>=
set.seed(42)
idat <- expand.grid(
    block = factor(seq_len(8)),
    habitat = factor(c("grass", "heath")),
    treatment = factor(c("control", "low", "high"),
                       levels = c("control", "low", "high"))
)
idat <- subset(idat, !(block %in% c("7", "8") & habitat == "heath"))
iY <- matrix(rpois(nrow(idat) * 6, lambda = 5), ncol = 6)
iY[, 1] <- iY[, 1] + 3 * (idat$treatment == "high")
iY[, 2] <- iY[, 2] + 3 * (idat$habitat == "heath")
iY[, 3] <- iY[, 3] +
    4 * (idat$treatment == "high" & idat$habitat == "heath")
imod <- rda(iY ~ treatment * habitat, data = idat)

set.seed(314)
iper <- shuffleSet(nrow(idat), nset = 99,
                   control = how(blocks = idat$block))
@

By default, \code{newdata = NULL} asks what the fitted response would be
if every observation had received each treatment in turn, while its
other variables kept their observed values. It then averages over
those observations. The
following result is therefore an average treatment comparison over the
observed habitats:

<<interaction-average>>=
ordicomparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "control", permutations = iper)
@

The \code{by} argument instead constructs treatment profiles within
each habitat. These rows make the simple effects and their ordering
visible before a test is specified.

<<interaction-by>>=
isc <- ordicontrasts(imod, "treatment", by = "habitat")
isc
@

Here \code{Averaged over: none} is expected. Habitat is displayed as a
grouping column, so it is held at the value in each row rather than
averaged over. The built-in comparisons are applied separately within each
habitat:

<<interaction-by-comparisons>>=
ordicomparisons(isc, comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            permutations = iper)
@

More than one variable may be named in \code{by}; each observed
combination becomes a group. Other model variables that vary within
those groups are still listed under \code{Averaged over}.

An explicit \code{newdata} data frame asks about a chosen population or
setting. Here treatment comparisons are evaluated only in \code{heath}. The
factor is created with the levels used in the fitted model.

<<interaction-newdata>>=
heath <- data.frame(
    habitat = factor("heath", levels = levels(idat$habitat))
)
heath.sc <- ordicontrasts(imod, "treatment", newdata = heath)
heath.sc
ordicomparisons(heath.sc, comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            permutations = iper)
@

Supplying several rows in \code{newdata} averages over those rows; it
does not automatically produce a separate result for each row. Use
\code{by} when separate results are wanted.

The profile identifiers can select simple effects directly. From the
printed table, high--control is \code{b3 - b1} in grass and
\code{b6 - b4} in heath:

<<two-simple-effects>>=
ordicomparisons(isc,
            comparison = c(grass = "b3 - b1 = 0",
                           heath = "b6 - b4 = 0"),
            permutations = iper)
@

They can also be combined into a difference-in-differences test:

<<difference-in-differences>>=
ordicomparisons(isc,
            comparison = "(b6 - b4) - (b3 - b1) = 0",
            permutations = iper)
@

The \code{b} indices cover all profile rows in their printed order.
Always inspect that table before writing an expression that crosses
groups.

The same approach extends to interactions among three or more variables:
use \code{by} or \code{newdata} to specify the combinations of the other
variables at which treatment levels should be compared, then compare
the resulting simple effects.

\section{Choosing values for the other model variables}

The meaning of a comparison in a model with other predictors depends on
the values over which those predictors are averaged. The available
choices are summarised below.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{0.23\textwidth}p{0.29\textwidth}p{0.37\textwidth}}
\toprule
Option & Categorical variables & Numeric variables \\
\midrule
\code{NULL} & Retain their observed values and frequencies &
  Retain their observed values \\
\code{"balanced"} & Use every level combination with equal weight &
  Fix at their arithmetic means \\
\code{"mean"} & Fix at their most frequent levels (modes) &
  Fix at their arithmetic means \\
\code{data.frame(...)} & Use the values and frequencies supplied by the user &
  Use the values supplied by the user \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Thus, \code{NULL} averages over the observed sample, replacing only the
factor being compared. The \code{"balanced"} option gives equal weight
to every combination of the other factor levels, with numeric variables
held at their means. An explicit data
frame describes a chosen population or setting and must contain the
variables needed to reconstruct the fitted model terms, apart from the
focal factor, which \code{ordicomparisons()} supplies in turn.

Because grass is more frequent than heath in this example,
\code{newdata = "mean"} asks for treatment comparisons at the modal
habitat, grass. Numeric predictors, if present, would be fixed at their
means. This is a comparison for one typical profile, not an average over
habitats:

<<mean-grid>>=
mean.sc <- ordicontrasts(imod, "treatment", newdata = "mean")
mean.sc
ordicomparisons(mean.sc, comparison = "b3 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = iper)
@

With \code{by}, means and modes are calculated separately within
each group. If factor levels are tied for the mode, the first tied level
in the fitted factor-level order is used.

The balanced grid instead gives grass and heath equal weight.
Printing the profiles shows the population used:

<<balanced-grid>>=
balanced.sc <- ordicontrasts(imod, "treatment", newdata = "balanced")
balanced.sc
ordicomparisons(balanced.sc, comparison = "b3 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = iper)
@

\medskip

Use \code{wts} when rows should contribute unequally. Supply finite,
non-negative numbers or name a numeric column in \code{newdata}.
Weights are normalised within each \code{by} group, and each group
needs at least one positive weight. In this artificial
example, heath observations receive twice the averaging weight of grass
observations:

<<weighted>>=
ipop <- idat[!duplicated(idat[c("block", "habitat")]),
             c("block", "habitat")]
ipop$sampling_weight <- ifelse(ipop$habitat == "heath", 2, 1)
weighted.sc <- ordicontrasts(imod, "treatment", newdata = ipop,
                               wts = "sampling_weight")
weighted.sc
ordicomparisons(weighted.sc, comparison = "b3 - b1 = 0",
            permutations = iper)
@

The weights control only averaging of fitted profiles. They are not
observation weights in the fitted ordination model.

An explicit numeric value lets us compare factor levels while holding
that variable fixed. The observed range of \code{A1} is 2.8 to 11.5, so
\code{A1 = 5} is within the fitted data range. It is shown under
\code{Fixed at}, and \code{Extrapolated} is false:

<<numeric-setting>>=
xmod <- rda(dune ~ Management * A1, data = dune.env)
range(dune.env$A1)
within.sc <- ordicontrasts(xmod, "Management",
                             newdata = data.frame(A1 = 5))
within.sc
@

Values outside that range are allowed, but are flagged. Here
\code{A1 = 12.5} lies above the observed maximum, so every displayed
profile has \code{Extrapolated = TRUE}:

<<extrapolation>>=
outside.sc <- ordicontrasts(xmod, "Management",
                              newdata = data.frame(A1 = 12.5))
outside.sc
@

These results rely on extending the fitted relationship beyond the
observed data, so they should be interpreted cautiously.

Transformations retain the settings used in the fitted model. For
example, a polynomial fitted with \code{poly()} keeps the same basis
when comparisons are made at the mean of \code{A1}:

<<fitted-transformations>>=
pmod <- rda(dune ~ Management + poly(A1, 2), data = dune.env)
ordicomparisons(pmod, "Management", comparison = "HF - BF = 0",
               newdata = "mean", permutations = perm)
@

Similarly, \code{scale()} reuses its fitted centre and scale. Fixed
expressions such as \code{I(A1\textasciicircum{}2)} are evaluated as written. These
settings are kept even when a subset of the data was used for fitting.

\section{Accounting for other variables and restricting permutations}

Original \code{Condition()} terms are retained in every comparison test.
Here moisture is partialled out before management comparisons are
tested in correspondence analysis:

<<cca-condition>>=
cmod <- cca(dune ~ Management + Condition(Moisture), data = dune.env)
ordicomparisons(cmod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm)
@

Permutation restrictions can be supplied in any form accepted by
vegan's permutation tests. A predefined permutation matrix,
as used throughout this vignette, is particularly useful because it is
reused for all rows. Alternatively, \code{strata} restricts
permutations to blocks:

<<strata-example,eval=false>>=
ordicomparisons(imod, "treatment", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "control", permutations = 99,
            strata = idat$block)
@

The \code{parallel} argument is passed to \code{permutest.cca()}. At
present, comparisons are processed one after another. If parallel
processing is requested, it applies to the permutations within each
test. An integer gives the number of workers; an existing socket
cluster may also be supplied. Tests run serially by default, unless
the user has changed the \code{mc.cores} option.

\section{Comparisons for CCA, distance-based RDA, and PERMANOVA}

The user interface is shared by RDA, CCA, and distance-based RDA. The
meaning of inertia follows the fitted method: variance for RDA,
chi-square inertia for CCA, and squared-distance inertia for dbRDA. CCA
also uses its weighted chi-square geometry in constructing the
comparison test.

<<dbrda>>=
dmod <- dbrda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env,
              distance = "bray")
ordicomparisons(dmod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm)
@

For PERMANOVA, the default return value of \code{adonis2()} remains its
ANOVA table. Request \code{return = "model"} when fitting if comparisons
will be needed afterwards:

<<adonis-model>>=
afit <- adonis2(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env,
                method = "bray", return = "model")
ordicomparisons(afit, "Management", comparison = "trt.vs.ctrl",
            reference = "BF", permutations = perm)
@

Retaining the model requires more memory than returning the usual
\code{adonis2()} table, so it must be requested explicitly. With
non-Euclidean dissimilarities, the tests use the same treatment of
negative eigenvalues as \code{dbrda()}. Their results describe differences
in the fitted dissimilarity representation; they do not estimate changes
in the abundance of individual species.

\section{Requesting comparisons through ANOVA}

You can also request these comparisons through \code{anova()} using
\code{by = "contrast"}. This uses the same calculations as
\code{ordicomparisons()} and returns the results in an ANOVA table:

<<anova-bridge>>=
anova(mod, by = "contrast", variable = "Management",
      comparison = "reference", reference = "BF",
      permutations = perm)
@

The result uses the familiar \code{anova.cca()} layout. The full
\code{ordicomparisons()} interface is preferable
when reference populations, grouped or cross-group comparisons, or
adjustments for multiple testing are needed. Use \code{ordicontrasts()}
to inspect profiles before writing custom expressions. Existing \code{by = "onedf"}
tests have a different
purpose: they are sequential tests of the columns produced by the
factor coding. Their meaning can therefore change with the chosen
factor coding.

\section{Current limitations and result attributes}

These comparisons use the ordinary pseudo-$F$ statistic. The modified-$F$
option available in \code{adonis2()} and \code{anova.cca()} supports
overall group tests; it does not yet support these contrast-specific tests.

The following restrictions currently apply:

\begin{itemize}
  \item exactly one focal factor is named in \code{variable};
  \item the model must have been fitted with the formula interface and
    must not contain redundant explanatory variables (an aliased
    constraint matrix);
  \item character expressions must be linear and have zero on the
    right-hand side;
  \item ratios between fitted levels, nonlinear functions,
    inequalities, equivalence regions, and non-zero null values are not
    supported;
  \item factor levels and interaction cells needed for a comparison
    must be represented well enough in the data to estimate the requested
    differences;
  \item separate tests use separate contrast-specific null models, even
    though they share a permutation matrix.
\end{itemize}

The returned object retains information useful for checking an
analysis. For example:

<<attributes>>=
names(attributes(pw))
attr(pw, "control")
attr(pw, "extrapolation")
attr(pw, "contrasts")
@

The \code{contrasts} attribute contains the \code{ordicontrasts()} object
used to define the comparisons, including its reference profiles and
averaging settings. Its \code{Profile} column identifies the displayed
rows, and its \code{profile.matrix} attribute retains their model-matrix
representation. The \code{comparison.matrices} attribute of the test
result contains these rows under \code{profiles} and the rows defining
the tested differences under \code{comparisons}. This information can
help check an analysis or develop new methods. Most users can specify
their comparisons through the function arguments without editing these
matrices.

\appendix
\section{How the comparison tests are calculated}

This appendix describes why the tests are invariant to the coding of a
factor and how the requested differences define a restricted model.
The coefficients below describe the fitted model mathematically;
individual coefficients are not reported or tested.

\subsection{Defining the hypothesis to be tested}

For RDA, write the fitted model as $Y=XB+Z\Gamma+E$, where $X$ contains
the constraints, $Z$ contains any original conditioning variables, and
$E$ contains the errors. The intercept is handled separately.

For each factor level $\ell$, \code{ordicontrasts()} builds model-matrix
rows at that level, keeping the other variables at their reference
values $v_i$. Its averaged row is
\[
  r_\ell^\mathsf{T}=\sum_i w_i x(\ell,v_i)^\mathsf{T},
  \qquad \sum_i w_i=1.
\]
Weights are normalised within each \code{by} group. Model-matrix rows
are constructed before averaging, so this need not equal evaluating
the model at the means of the original variables. Fitted settings for
supported transformations, such as \code{poly()} and \code{scale()},
are reused rather than recalculated from the reference data.

Put these rows into $R$. A matrix $A$ specifies comparisons between
them, giving
\[
  L=AR, \qquad H_0:LB=0.
\]
For example, \code{"b2 - b1 = 0"} supplies weights $(-1,1,0,\ldots)$
to a row of $A$. Each row sums to zero, so the intercept and common
centring constants cancel. Reference rows define the hypothesis; the
test still uses the original fitted observations and response.

For a change of coding represented by an invertible matrix $T$,
$X^\ast=XT$, $R^\ast=RT$,
$L^\ast=LT$, and $B^\ast=T^{-1}B$. Hence $L^\ast B^\ast=LB$:
the hypothesis is unchanged. Intercept shifts introduced by recoding
also cancel because the comparison weights sum to zero.

\subsection{Separating the requested difference from other model effects}

The fitted QR decomposition gives $X_\perp=Q_X S$ for the centred
constraints after removing the original conditions. Here $Q_X$ has
orthonormal columns and $S$ is triangular. Columns of $L$ are placed in
the same order as the constraints in the decomposition. Writing
$C=SB$ gives $X_\perp B=Q_X C$, so the hypothesis is
$(LS^{-1})C=0$. The code computes $S^{-\mathsf{T}}L^\mathsf{T}$ with
a triangular solve.

A second QR decomposition separates these coordinates into tested
directions $M$ and null directions $N$. The columns of $N$ satisfy
$LS^{-1}N=0$. The test conditions on $Z$ and $Q_X N$, and uses
$Q_X M$ as the tested directions. These two spaces are orthogonal in
observation space. The tested space is therefore the same under
equivalent codings, including when its columns are passed to the
permutation test.

Together the spaces span the full fitted design. The residual inertia
and degrees of freedom therefore remain those of the full model.
Let $q$ be the number of tested directions and $df_E$ the residual
degrees of freedom.
If $I_{E,0}$ and $I_{E,1}$ are the restricted- and full-model residual
inertias, the contrast inertia and statistic are

\[
  I_L=I_{E,0}-I_{E,1}, \qquad
  F=\frac{I_L/q}{I_{E,1}/df_E}.
\]
For one-response RDA, this is the usual partial $F$ statistic, although
its permutation $p$-value need not equal the parametric $p$-value.

The code projects the transformed response from \code{ordiYbar()}
directly onto the tested and residual spaces to calculate their inertias.
The degrees of freedom come from the design. A contrast with zero
inertia can be tested if the model has residual inertia.
CCA uses row masses (row totals divided by the
overall total) to define the projections and uses chi-square inertia.
These masses are distinct from the averaging weights above. For dbRDA,
inertia is the trace of the relevant fitted or residual part of
$G=-\tfrac12 HD^{(2)}H$, where $H$ centres the rows and columns and
$D^{(2)}$ contains the squared dissimilarities used in fitting. Contributions from negative
eigenvalues are retained.

\subsection{Calculating the permutation p-value}

The test passes the design and inertia to \code{permutest.cca()} with
\code{model = "reduced"}.
This uses vegan's existing procedure for permuting residuals after
accounting for the conditioning variables. A tested direction that lies
in the permuted conditioning space contributes zero inertia to that
permutation. The numerator and denominator are recalculated for each
permutation. With $K$ permutations,
\[
  p=\frac{1+\#\{F^{(b)}\geq F_{obs}\}}{1+K}.
\]
The permutation scheme must reflect the sampling or experimental
design: null-model errors must be exchangeable under the permitted
permutations, for example within specified blocks. Estimating the other
model effects means that residual-permutation tests are not automatically
exact for every design \citep{Winkler2014}.

Separate comparisons reuse one permutation matrix but have their own
restricted models. With \code{joint = TRUE}, their $L$ rows are combined
and tested once using their joint rank. Expressions combine these rows
before testing; they never combine calculated inertias or statistics.
Only linear comparisons with a zero null are currently supported,
because these define the restricted linear model used here.

